remstart
    SCHOOLS
    another hidden thing, and a prototype. the routines are in fish_routines_school, and the tests are
    in fish_tests_school.

    FACING. every fish faces left or right. a new fish comes in facing one way or the other, picked by
    the seed and how many fish have come in so far, so a seed plays the same, and no roll is taken from
    the streams. a swap that goes across turns the two fish that it moves: the fish that the cursor
    carries faces the way that it was carried, and the fish that it shoves faces the other way. a swap
    that goes up or down leaves them facing the way that they were. a "free move" turns them the same as
    any swap does. the special moves do not. a fish takes the way that it faces along with it, when it
    is swapped or falls: swapCellStacks, in fish_funcs, trades it.
    the picture of a fish faces one way, and SCHOOL_ART_FACE says which. a fish that faces the other way
    is drawn turned around, and it turns slowly, so a swap that turns it can be seen. frame_fish_fx adds
    SCHOOL_SHOWN# to the yaw. a crab, a starfish, a jellyfish, a stingray and a bottle look the same from either side,
    so the way that they face cannot be seen, and they are never in a school. crabs walk sideways anyway.

    A SCHOOL. a group of fish that matched is a school when all of these are so:
        none of its fish is a crab, or one of the others that look the same from either side. it can
            be any shape.
        every fish of it faces the same way
        it has SCHOOL_MIN fish, or more
        the swap that led to this look at the board put one of its two fish in it. a group that only
            fell together is never a school. SCHOOL_NEED_SWAP turns that off, for the debug panel.
    THE CARD. "fall in line", a passive. with it, a group of fish that fell together, as the board filled
    back in, can be a school too, and not only one that a swap made: see SCHOOL_NEED_SWAP. it comes
    after the cards of the fast and the angler, like the angler it is dealt, and like them it is not
    written in the log of a game, which has no room for it. Tools/fall_in_line_art_gen.py draws its
    picture.

    THREE MORE CARDS. they come after "fall in line", and they are dealt too.
      "flipper", a move, is a swap like any other, and then both fish turn around. across, each one faces
        the other way from the way that a swap would turn it; up or down, each one turns from the way that
        it faced. schoolFlipped does it.
      "high stakes", a move, is a swap like any other, whose points are doubled: the swap, the fish that
        fall and pop after it, and the fish of a school that it pops, out in the background. SCHOOL_STAKES
        is 2 from the swap until the next swap or special move, and every flushed fish keeps the stakes
        it was flushed under in FLOCK_STAKES. the multiplier shows the doubled number, in gold.
      "tolerance", a passive. when the cursor steps off a fish and sends its school away, a third of the
        school stays, rounded down: the fish nearest the middle of the screen. schoolDepart does it.
    Tools/school_cards_art_gen.py draws their pictures.

    THE DAWN. at daybreak, every group that the night held pops at once, and none of them was made by a
    swap just then, but by the moves of the night. so on that look at the board, every one of them that
    faces one way is a school, whatever made it: the night is for setting the board up, and that goes
    for schools too. tideDayBreaks, in fish_routines_tide, sets SCHOOL_DAWN, and the end of the look
    clears it, so the fish that fall in after that are judged the usual way.
    by chance, three fish would face the same way one time in four, and four of them one time in eight.
    the swap has to have made it so that it does not happen too often without the player meaning it to.

    A SCHOOL SWIMS OFF. a school does not pop. it scores the same as if it had, and it leaves the board
    the same way, but there is no puff and no bang: each of its fish fades off of the board as a fish
    just like it lifts off and swims away, slowly, the way that the school faced, out into the water
    behind the board. it gets smaller as it goes, as if it were going further away, a blue haze comes
    over it, and the dark line around it fades, the way that it would be lost in the water far off.
    FlockEffect.fx does the line. the bubbles still come up from where it was, and in place of the
    bang, a chord rings. schoolFishLeaves does the rest of it, from where handle_combos would have made
    the puff, and schoolChord the chord.

    THE FANFARE OF THE SCHOOLS. every school, of any kind, plays a short fanfare: a chord, rolled quickly
    from the bottom note up, with its root again an octave higher on top. a school of
    SCHOOL_FANFARE_BIG fish or more adds one more note, higher still. every school plays the next chord
    of a run of SCHOOL_FANFARE_LENGTH of them, and when SCHOOL_SONG_RESET_MS go by with no school, the
    run starts again from the first. it is played on SCHOOL_CHORD_INSTRUMENT.
    the music has a chord under it all of the time, D minor or B flat (see MUSIC_CHORD in fish_headers),
    and its tunes are written over that chord. so there are two runs, one of chords that sit well over
    D minor, and one of chords that sit well over B flat, and a school plays from whichever one goes
    with the chord that is up. that way it does not clash with the music, or with a tune that is
    playing. setup_school writes the two runs, in SCHOOL_FANFARE_ROOT. a chord is its root and every
    other note of the scale above it, and the notes are the ones in fish_headers. a new game, and a
    game that is loaded, start the run from the first chord.

    THE SCHOOLS IN THE BACKGROUND. the fish that have swum off stay, out in the water behind the board,
    and every one of them joins the others of its kind: a school of every kind of fish that has been
    made into one. they flock, the way that real fish do. every fish keeps a little way from the fish
    of its kind that are closest, swims the way that they swim, and keeps close to them, and it keeps
    well away from fish of any other kind, so the schools stay apart. a fish that is a long way from
    its school is drawn back to it. none of them leave the screen. they would rather not swim over the
    board: one that is over it is pushed toward the nearer side of it, gently, so one can be seen
    crossing behind the fish now and then, or be made to by the others, but it does not stay there.
    this is only to be looked at, for now.
    there are FLOCK_MAX of them at the most. when another one comes, the one that has been out there
    the longest goes. a new game starts with none. a fish turns around when it starts to swim the
    other way: it narrows to nothing and opens out again facing the other way, the way that the fish on
    the board turn. they are kept with the saved game: see THE SAVED GAME, below.
    THE SCHOOLS SLEEP AT NIGHT. as night comes on, the fish out there stop swimming, and each one just
    drifts, slowly, a little this way and that, in place, the way a fish asleep does. as day comes
    back, they swim on from where they were. it follows how much of the night is drawn,
    TIDE_NIGHT_SHOWN# in fish_headers_tide, so it comes and goes smoothly. they are not worked out
    while it is all the way night, since they are not going anywhere.

    A POP SENDS A WAVE through them. when a group of fish pops on the board, a ring goes out from the
    middle of it, across the water behind the board, and every fish out there that it passes is pushed
    straight away from the pop: harder the closer it was, and harder for a bigger group. a fish that is
    still on its way out is left alone, so the fish of a school go off calmly. the push fades
    by itself, since the next time that a fish is worked out it is brought back down to the fastest
    that a fish swims, and then it swims back to its school. so a pop sends a ripple through the
    schools. flockWave starts one, where handle_combos sends the shockwave of a group.

    the flock is worked out a few fish at a time, FLOCK_STEER_EACH of them on a frame, since every
    fish has to look at every other one. that is when a fish changes how fast it goes, and which way:
    for the rest of the time it just goes. they all move on every frame. this is slow work for the
    script, so a frame does as little for each fish as it can, and the sprite of a fish that is all
    the way out is only told where it is, and how far around it is turned while it turns.
    LATER: cards may change what a school does. one could make it swim off through the fish in its
    way, popping them, which is what SCHOOL_SWEEP does: in every row that the school has a fish in,
    every fish ahead of it, from its front in that row to the edge of the board, pops too, as one
    forced group for that row. it goes past the diver. see FORCED POPS in fish_headers: a school in
    a row takes FORCE_SCHOOL and the row. schoolJudge does it, where handle_combos shoots the stingrays.
    SCHOOL_SWEEP is 0, for now.

    THE CURSOR CALLS ON THE SCHOOLS. every time that the cursor comes to a fish, by a step or by
    carrying it in a swap, that is a visit. while it is there, the school of that kind of fish, out in
    the background, waits on it:
        a swap that makes a match, any match, pops every fish of the kind that the cursor carried in
            it, out in the background, one by one, where they are: each one punches and pops, the same
            as in a flush (see below), and scores 10 more than the last, times the multiplier. but a
            swap that makes a new school of that kind does not: the new fish join the school out there,
            and it grows. so a school is grown with schools, and cashed in with any other match that the
            carried fish is part of. a swap whose match is all of the fish that the cursor shoved back
            does nothing to them: they do not pop, and they do not go, and stepping off after it is safe.
        stepping off of the fish, without a swap that made a match there, sends every fish of its
            kind out in the background away. they swim off the side of the screen that is nearer, at
            an easy pace, and they do not come back. there are no points, and nothing is made of it,
            but over the first SCHOOL_LEAVE_FADE_MS they get smaller, darker and fainter, so that a
            player who looks can see that they are gone. a step to a fish of the same kind is not
            stepping off of it: the visit goes on, so the cursor can move between two of them. and
            stepping off of a dead fish, belly up or bones, is no mistake: nothing is at stake on it.
        while the cursor is on a fish, the fish of its kind out in the background come a little
            forward: a little bigger, a good way clear of the haze, and shimmering a little, the haze
            coming and going over each of them in a time of its own. that is how it can be seen what
            is at stake. nothing comes forward for a dead fish.
    the score that a fish out there pops for is as far off as the fish: the same haze over its color,
    and only as much of it shows as of the fish.
            a swap that makes no match does not count: the cursor carried the fish, so it is still on
            it, and stepping off of it after that sends them away.
    so the schools are a store of points, and the cursor is how they are spent, or lost.
    schoolSwapMade and schoolCursorStepped hear about the swaps and the steps, from stepLiveMove in
    fish_funcs. schoolJudgeSwap pops them, from handle_combos. schoolDepart sends them away.

    THE TIDE FLUSHES THE SCHOOLS. this is turned off for now, by SCHOOL_FLUSH_ON. the tide always wants one thing next, a push or a pull: the specks
    in the water say which. a swap that is what the tide wants flushes every fish out in the
    background. a push sends them up, and a pull sends them down. each one leaves its school and swims
    off that way, faster and faster, and they pop, one by one, in an order picked at random, spread over
    SCHOOL_FLUSH_SPAN, and never later than when one gets to the edge of the screen. when its turn comes,
    a fish punches: it swells quickly to SCHOOL_PUNCH_SCALE of its size and comes clear of the haze, and
    then it pops the way that the fish of the board do, with one of their puffs of smoke, out in the
    water behind the board, and small bubbles. the sound is the pop of the board heard far off through
    the water, made by Tools/school_pop_gen.py. and it scores. the first one to pop is worth 10, the
    next 20, and so on, times the multiplier, the way that the fish of a group are, and the points go
    onto the bar the same way. so the schools are a store of points, and the tide is how they are let
    go. nothing pops at night, and the tide does not move then, so there is no flush at night.
    judgeTide, in fish_routines_tide, calls schoolTideSwap, before it moves the water. schoolFlush
    sends them, and schoolFlushPop pops one.

    THE FISH COME HOME. this is turned off for now, by SCHOOL_DRAW_ON, and it may come back on a card.
    a swap that goes down calls on the schools. from then on, until a swap goes up,
    the new fish that drop in to fill the board come from the schools out in the background, where
    they can: one of the fish out there, of a kind that does not pop as soon as it lands, swims over
    to the column, as small and hazy as it was, and waits in a stack above it, with the others that
    are going into that column: the one for the lowest cell at the bottom, just above the board. one
    after the other, from the bottom up, each one hops in: it drops a little, grows to the size of the fish on the board and fades away, as the new
    fish fades in and falls. it has left its school for good. a fish nearer the top of the screen is
    picked more often: each one that would do has a chance that grows with how high up it is, squared.
    a fish that is still on its way out to its school is never called on. a swap that goes up sends them back to being picked at random, the way
    that they always were, and that is how a game starts. a swap that goes across changes nothing.
    a fish from a school may make a pair where it lands, which a fish picked at random nearly never
    may, and that is what the schools give. it may not make a whole group, so it never pops right away.
    only the plain kinds of fish are called on: a jellyfish, a stingray, a bottle, the fish that a
    "known fill" swap brings, and the fish that a quest brings, drop in as they always do.
    THEY ARE PICKED EARLY, so that they have time to swim over without the board waiting for them. as
    soon as handle_combos knows how far the fish that are left will fall, before anything moves, it is
    known which cells will be empty: the top cells of every column, as many as popped in it. a fish
    from the schools is picked for each of them then, against the board as it will be once the fish
    have fallen, and it sets off at once. schoolEarlyPicks does that. it takes SCHOOL_DRAW_FLY to swim
    over, which is about as long as the fish take to fall, and it waits above the column if it is early.
    as the board fills back in, schoolDrawTake looks again, at the board as it really is: the fish that
    was picked for the cell at random has to be a plain one, and the fish from the school must not make
    a whole group there with the new fish that dropped in beside it. if it can not drop in after all,
    it goes back to its school, and the fish that was picked at random drops in. if it can,
    schoolDrawLand has the new fish fall when it would have, or once the fish from the school is there,
    if that is later, and the fish from the school hops in as it starts to. where the swap
    goes is told to schoolDrawWay, by startLiveMove in fish_funcs.
    which fish out there is called on goes around them in turn, so a seed does not always play the
    same once a game has schools in it: where the fish swim does not come from the seed.

    THE SAVED GAME. the way that every fish on the board faces, and the fish out in the background, are
    kept with the saved game, from SCHOOL_PREF on. a game that
    was saved before any of this deals the fish a way to face, and has nobody out in the background.
    schoolSave and schoolLoad, which fish_routines_save calls.

    the "Schools" window of the debug panel (FISH_DEBUG) turns it off and on, sets how big a school has
    to be, and turns every fish one way, the other way, or back to the way that it was dealt.
remend

global SCHOOL_ON = 1 `0 turns the whole thing off
global SCHOOL_MIN = 3 `how many fish a school needs, at the least
global dim SCHOOL_MADE(64) `1 for every kind that a school was made of, on this look at the board. schoolJudgeSwap clears them.
#constant ABILITY_INDEX_FALL_IN_LINE 36
#constant ABILITY_INDEX_FLIPPER 37
#constant ABILITY_INDEX_HIGH_STAKES 38
#constant ABILITY_INDEX_TOLERANCE 39
global SCHOOL_STAKES = 1 `2 while the points of a "high stakes" swap are doubled. see THREE MORE CARDS.
global dim FLOCK_STAKES(48) `the stakes that every fish out in the background was flushed under
global dim SCHOOL_STAY(48) `1 for every fish out in the background that "tolerance" keeps, when its school is sent away
global SCHOOL_DAWN = 0 `1 for the look at the board that day breaks for, when any group can be a school. see THE DAWN.
global SCHOOL_NEED_SWAP = 1 `1 when only a group that the last swap put together can be a school
global FORCE_SCHOOL = 120 `the fish ahead of a school that swims off. the row is added to this. see FORCED POPS in fish_headers.

global dim SCHOOL_FACE(400) `which way the fish in every cell faces: 1 is to the right, and -1 to the left
global dim SCHOOL_SHOWN#(400) `how far around the fish in every cell is drawn, from 0, the way that its picture faces, to 1, turned around
global dim SCHOOL_ART_FACE(64) `which way the picture of every kind of fish faces: 1 to the right, -1 to the left, and 0 for one that looks the same from either side. setup_school fills it in.
global SCHOOL_SNAP = 1 `1 when every fish should be drawn facing its way right away, without turning. a new game does that.
global SCHOOL_TURN_STEP# = 0 `how much of the way a fish turns on this frame. stepSchool works it out.
global SCHOOL_TURN_EASE# = 0.008 `how much of the way it turns in a millisecond
global SCHOOL_LAST_MS = 0
global SCHOOL_SPAWNS = 0 `how many fish have come in this game. it goes into which way the next one faces.

` what has happened, for the debug panel
global SCHOOL_COUNT = 0 `how many schools have swum off in this game
global SCHOOL_LAST_SIZE = 0 `how many fish the last one had
global SCHOOL_LAST_SWEPT = 0 `and how many it popped on its way off
global dim SCHOOL_ROW_LEFT(64) `scratch, for schoolJudge: the leftmost fish of the group in every row, or -1 for none
global dim SCHOOL_ROW_RIGHT(64) `and the rightmost

global SCHOOL_SWEEP = 0 `1 when a school swims off through the fish in its way, popping them. see LATER, above.
global SCHOOL_JUDGED = -1 `the group that schoolJudge last found to be a school, or -1. handle_combos asks about it as it pops the group.

` THE SCHOOLS IN THE BACKGROUND
global FLOCK_MAX = 48 `how many fish there can be out there at once
global dim FLOCK_ON(48) `1 for a fish that is out there
global dim FLOCK_KIND(48) `the kind of fish that it is
global dim FLOCK_X#(48) `where it is, in pixels
global dim FLOCK_Y#(48)
global dim FLOCK_VX#(48) `how fast it is going, in pixels a second
global dim FLOCK_VY#(48)
global dim FLOCK_AX#(48) `how it was steering, the last time that it was worked out, in pixels a second, every second
global dim FLOCK_AY#(48)
global dim FLOCK_BORN(48) `when it left the board, on the clock of the game. it is not there until then.
global dim FLOCK_FACE(48) `which way it faces: 1 to the right, and -1 to the left
global dim FLOCK_SPRITES(48)
global FLOCK_STEER_NEXT = 0 `the fish that is worked out next
` THE CURSOR CALLS ON THE SCHOOLS. see above.
global SCHOOL_CURSOR_ON = 1 `0 turns it off, for the debug panel
global SCHOOL_PENDING = 0 `1 when a swap has been made, and has not been judged yet
global SCHOOL_PENDING_KIND = 0 `the kind of fish that the cursor carried in it
global SCHOOL_SWAP_VALID = 0 `1 when handle_combos has seen a group that the swap put together
global SCHOOL_CARRIED_MATCHED = 0 `1 when the fish that the cursor carried in it is part of such a group
global SCHOOL_VISIT_VALID = 0 `1 when a swap made a match since the cursor came to the fish that it is on
global dim FLOCK_LEAVE(48) `-1 for a fish that is leaving off the left of the screen, 1 for the right, and 0 for one that is not
global SCHOOL_LEAVE_SPEED = 35 `how fast a fish that is leaving swims, in pixels a second
global dim FLOCK_LEAVE_AT(48) `when it started to leave, on the clock of the game
global SCHOOL_LEAVE_FADE_MS = 750 `how long a fish that is leaving takes to get further off
global SCHOOL_LEAVE_SHRINK = 30 `how much smaller it gets, in hundredths of its size
global SCHOOL_LEAVE_FADE = 45 `and how much fainter, in hundredths
global dim FLOCK_FOCUS#(48) `how far it has come forward, from 0 to 1, while the cursor is on a fish of its kind
global SCHOOL_FOCUS_KIND = 0 `the kind of fish that the cursor is on, on this frame, or 0
global SCHOOL_FOCUS_EASE# = 0.006 `how much of the way it comes forward, or goes back, in a millisecond
global SCHOOL_FOCUS_GROW = 15 `how much bigger it gets when it comes forward, in hundredths of its size
global SCHOOL_FOCUS_CLEAR = 50 `and how much of the haze comes off of it, in hundredths
global SCHOOL_SHIMMER = 20 `and while it is forward, how much more of the haze comes and goes, as it shimmers, in hundredths
global SCHOOL_SHIMMER_SPEED = 600 `how quickly it shimmers, in hundred-thousandths of a turn a millisecond
global SCHOOL_DEPART_COUNT = 0 `how many fish have left in this game, for the debug panel
global SCHOOL_FLUSH_ONLY_KIND = 0 `scratch, for schoolPopKind: the only kind that a flush takes, or 0 for all of them

` THE TIDE FLUSHES THE SCHOOLS. see above.
global SCHOOL_FLUSH_ON = 0 `1 turns it on. it is off for now.
global dim FLOCK_FLUSH(48) `-1 for a fish that the tide is flushing up, 1 for one it is flushing down, and 0 for one it is not
global SCHOOL_FLUSH_SPEED = 150 `how fast a flushed fish swims, at the most, in pixels a second
global SCHOOL_FLUSH_PUSH = 260 `how quickly it gets up to that, in pixels a second, every second
global dim FLOCK_FLUSH_AT(48) `when a flushed fish pops, on the clock of the game, if it has not got to the edge of the screen first
global dim SCHOOL_FLUSH_ORDER(48) `scratch, for schoolFlush: the fish of a flush, in the order that they pop
global dim FLOCK_PUNCH_AT(48) `when a flushed fish started its punch, on the clock of the game, or 0 before its turn
global SCHOOL_PUNCH_MS = 110 `how long the punch takes, in milliseconds. the fish pops at the end of it.
global SCHOOL_PUNCH_SCALE = 190 `how big it swells to, in hundredths of its size
global SCHOOL_PUFF_MAX = 10 `how many puffs of smoke there can be at once, out in the background
global dim SCHOOL_PUFF_SPRITES(10)
global dim SCHOOL_PUFF_START(10) `when each one started, on the clock of the game, or 0 for one that is over
global dim SCHOOL_PUFF_FROM(10) `the frames of the puff that it plays, from and to
global dim SCHOOL_PUFF_TO(10)
global SCHOOL_PUFF_NEXT = 0
global SCHOOL_PUFF_MS = 400 `how long a puff takes, the same as the board's
global SCHOOL_PUFF_ALPHA = 200 `how much of a puff shows, from 0 to 255
global SCHOOL_FLUSH_FIRST = 200 `how long after the flush the first one pops, in milliseconds
global SCHOOL_FLUSH_GAP = 120 `how far apart they pop, in milliseconds
global SCHOOL_FLUSH_SPAN = 1600 `and the longest that a whole flush takes to pop: a big one pops closer together
global SCHOOL_FAR_VOICES = 3 `how many of each far pop can be heard at once
global dim SCHOOL_FAR_SFX(6) `the sounds of the far pops: the voices of the first, and then of the second
global SCHOOL_FAR_NEXT = 0 `the voice that the next pop is played on
global SCHOOL_FLUSH_EDGE = 6 `how far from the top or the bottom of the screen it pops, in pixels
global SCHOOL_FLUSH_VOLUME = 35 `how loud a far pop is, in hundredths
global SCHOOL_FLUSH_SCORE = 0 `what the last fish of the flush that is going scored, before the multiplier. the next scores 10 more.
global SCHOOL_FLUSH_COUNT = 0 `how many flushes there have been in this game, for the debug panel

` THE FISH COME HOME. see above.
global SCHOOL_DRAW_ON = 0 `1 when a swap that goes down calls on the schools. it is off for now.
global SCHOOL_DRAW = 0 `1 after a swap that went down, when the new fish come from the schools
global SCHOOL_DRAW_NEXT = 0 `the fish out there that is looked at first, the next time that one is called on
global SCHOOL_STACK_LIFT = 110 `how far above the middle of the top row of the board the bottom of a stack waits, in hundredths of a cell
global SCHOOL_STACK_SPACE = 75 `how far apart the fish in a stack are, in hundredths of a cell
global SCHOOL_STACK_GAP = 110 `how long after one fish of a stack drops in the next one does, in milliseconds
global dim SCHOOL_COLUMN_EMPTY(32) `scratch, for schoolEarlyPicks: how many cells of every column will be empty
global dim FLOCK_STACK(48) `the place of a fish from a school in its stack above the column, 0 at the bottom
global SCHOOL_DRAW_FLY = 650 `how long a fish from a school takes to swim over to its column, in milliseconds
global dim SCHOOL_EARLY(400) `the fish out there that has been sent for every cell that will be empty, or -1
global dim SCHOOL_FUTURE(400) `scratch, for schoolEarlyPicks: the board as it will be once the fish have fallen
global dim SCHOOL_FUTURE_MARKS(400) `scratch, for schoolFutureGroup
global dim SCHOOL_FUTURE_STACK(400)
global SCHOOL_FUTURE_STAMP = 0
global dim FLOCK_HOP_AT(48) `when a fish from a school hops into its new fish, on the clock of the game, or 0 until that is known
global SCHOOL_DRAW_HOP = 120 `how long the one from the school takes to hop in, in milliseconds
global SCHOOL_PICK_TOP = 2 `how much a fish nearer the top is favored: 0 not at all, 1 by how high up it is, 2 by that squared
global SCHOOL_PICK_STAMP = 0 `scratch, for schoolDrawPick: a number that is new for every pick
global dim SCHOOL_PICK_ASKED(64) `the pick that each kind was last asked about on
global dim SCHOOL_PICK_FITS(64) `and whether it fits: 1 when it does not pop where it lands
global dim SCHOOL_PICK_WEIGHT(48) `the chance of every fish out there, on this pick
global SCHOOL_DRAW_COUNT = 0 `how many fish have come home in this game, for the debug panel
global dim FLOCK_FLY(48) `1 for a fish that is on its way to the board, to be a new fish
global dim FLOCK_FLY_X#(48) `where it set off from, in pixels
global dim FLOCK_FLY_Y#(48)
global dim FLOCK_FLY_TO_X#(48) `and where it is going
global dim FLOCK_FLY_TO_Y#(48)
global dim FLOCK_FLY_START(48) `when it set off, on the clock of the game
global dim FLOCK_FLY_MS(48) `and how long it takes

` THE SCHOOLS SLEEP AT NIGHT. see above.
global FLOCK_SLEEP_DRIFT = 15 `how far a sleeping fish drifts each way, in tenths of a pixel a second
global FLOCK_SLEEP_SLOW = 25 `how slowly it drifts back and forth, in hundredths of a turn a second

` A POP SENDS A WAVE. see above.
global FLOCK_WAVE_MAX = 8 `how many waves can be going at once. a new one takes the place of the oldest.
global dim FLOCK_WAVE_X#(8) `where each one started, in pixels
global dim FLOCK_WAVE_Y#(8)
global dim FLOCK_WAVE_START(8) `when it started, on the clock of the game, or 0 for one that is over
global dim FLOCK_WAVE_POWER#(8) `how hard it pushes, from the group that sent it
global FLOCK_WAVE_NEXT = 0
global FLOCK_WAVE_SPEED = 260 `how fast the ring goes out, in pixels a second
global FLOCK_WAVE_REACH = 360 `how far it gets before it is gone, in pixels
global FLOCK_WAVE_BAND = 22 `how thick the ring is, in pixels. a fish is pushed for as long as it is inside it.
global FLOCK_WAVE_PUSH = 350 `how hard it pushes a fish right next to the pop, in pixels a second, every second, for a group of three
global FLOCK_WAVE_GROW = 25 `how much harder it is for every fish more in the group, in hundredths
global FLOCK_STEER_EACH = 3 `how many fish are worked out on a frame. with FLOCK_MAX of them, each one is worked out every FLOCK_MAX / FLOCK_STEER_EACH frames.
global dim FLOCK_STEERED(48) `when each one was last worked out, on the clock of the game. the steer is used for all of the time since.
global dim FLOCK_DRAWN(48) `what its sprite has been told: 0 nothing yet, 1 while it is on its way out, and 2 once it is all the way out, after which its size, color and line are left alone
global dim FLOCK_SHOWING(48) `1 while its sprite is showing, so that it is only shown or hidden when that changes
global FLOCK_LOOK_SIG = -1 `what the sliders of the debug panel that change how they look were, the last frame
global FLOCK_LAST_MS = 0

global FLOCK_SIZE = 11 `how big a fish out there is drawn, in pixels. the fish on the board are CELL_ART_SIZE.
global FLOCK_ALPHA = 165 `how much of it can be seen, out there, from 0 to 255
global FLOCK_HAZE_R = 165 `the color that it takes on, which is the blue of the water far off
global FLOCK_HAZE_G = 195
global FLOCK_HAZE_B = 230
global FLOCK_SETTLE_MS = 1800 `how long it takes to swim from the board to out there
global FLOCK_LIFT_SPEED = 26 `how fast a fish swims off of the board, in pixels a second
global FLOCK_SPEED_MIN = 7 `how slowly a fish out there can swim, and how fast, in pixels a second
global FLOCK_SPEED_MAX = 22
global FLOCK_NEAR = 60 `how far away a fish of the same kind can be, and still be swum with, in pixels
global FLOCK_GAP = 14 `how close a fish lets one of its own kind get, in pixels
global FLOCK_AVOID = 46 `how close it lets a fish of another kind get, in pixels
global FLOCK_EDGE = 24 `how far from the edge of the screen it turns back, in pixels
global FLOCK_PULL_COHERE = 30 `how hard it steers toward the middle of the ones near it, in hundredths
global FLOCK_PULL_ALIGN = 50 `how hard it steers to swim the way that they do, in hundredths
global FLOCK_PULL_GAP = 900 `how hard it steers away from one of its own kind that is too close
global FLOCK_PULL_AVOID = 1400 `how hard it steers away from a fish of another kind that is too close
global FLOCK_PULL_HOME = 4 `how hard a fish far from its school steers back to it, in hundredths
global FLOCK_PULL_EDGE = 60 `how hard it steers away from the edge of the screen
global FLOCK_WANDER = 6 `how much it wanders on its own, in pixels a second, every second
global ORDER_FLOCK = -5 `on top of the sea floor, and underneath the coral, the specks and the fish
global FLOCK_FX_ID = 0 `FlockEffect.fx, which fades the line around a fish
global FLOCK_LINE_KEEP = 25 `how much of the line around a fish is left, all the way out, from 0 to 255
global FLOCK_PULL_GRID = 50 `how hard a fish over the board is pushed off of it, in pixels a second, every second
global FLOCK_GRID_MARGIN = 10 `and how far past the edge of the board that starts, in pixels
global FLOCK_GRID_L = 0 `the board, in pixels, worked out on every frame
global FLOCK_GRID_R = 0
global FLOCK_GRID_T = 0
global FLOCK_GRID_B = 0

` THE FANFARE OF THE SCHOOLS. see above.
global SCHOOL_FANFARE_LENGTH = 8 `how many chords a run has
global dim SCHOOL_FANFARE_ROOT(16) `the roots of the chords of the two runs: the one over D minor, and then the one over B flat
global SCHOOL_FANFARE_BIG = 5 `a school this big adds a note on top
global SCHOOL_SONG_AT = 0 `which chord of the run the next school plays
global SCHOOL_SONG_LAST = 0 `when the last school played, on the clock of the game
global SCHOOL_SONG_HEARD = 0 `1 once a school has played in this game
global SCHOOL_SONG_RESET_MS = 60000 `how long it takes with no school for the song to start again from the top
global SCHOOL_CHORD_INSTRUMENT = 0 `INSTRUMENT_BELL. see the instruments in fish_headers.
global SCHOOL_CHORD_VOLUME = 15 `how loud, in hundredths
global SCHOOL_NOTE_GAP = 125 `how far apart the notes of a fanfare are, in milliseconds, from the bottom up
global SCHOOL_NOTE_MAX = 16 `the most notes that a school plays
global dim SCHOOL_CHORD_NOTES(16) `the notes that a school is playing
global dim SCHOOL_CHORD_AT(16) `and when each of them is due, on the clock of the game, or 0 for one that has rung
global SCHOOL_CHORD_PLAYING = 0 `the instrument that they are on

` the fish out in the background turn around, too
global dim FLOCK_SHOWN#(48) `how far around a fish out there is drawn, from 0, the way that its picture faces, to 1, turned around
global FLOCK_TURN_EASE# = 0.005 `how much of the way it turns in a millisecond

` THE SAVED GAME. see above. these are the numbers from SCHOOL_PREF on.
global SCHOOL_PREF = 5000 `1 when the saved game has these
global SCHOOL_PREF_FLOCK = 5001 `how many fish are out in the background, and then one number for each of them
global SCHOOL_PREF_FACES = 5060 `the way that every fish on the board faces, 30 cells to a number
